From e767313cdb3a0ae6078d94c7710cc2421386d1bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:17:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDVideoCodecFFmpeg: decode both views of a stream of two
 views, as one picture

A stream marked as two views (H.264 MVC) played as its base view: the
H.264 decoder knows nothing of the second view, and Kodi shows a
stereoscopic video from one picture that holds both eyes.

Such a stream is now given to the decoder that outputs both views,
which is another decoder than the one of every other H.264 stream and
is asked for by its name ("h264_mvc"), with its option "view_ids" set
to all views. It decodes in software, with threads; no hardware decoder
is tried for it. Of that decoder this is relied upon: the option; one
picture for each view of an access unit, both with the presentation
time of that access unit (the decoder gives the second view the time of
the base view that it decoded it with, so a pair that the decoder
itself got wrong cannot be told from a right one here); the name of the
view on each picture when more than one view is output, the first
picture that has one being a base view; and that it can be flushed
before any packet.

The two pictures of an access unit are put side by side at their full
size (CMultiviewPacker), the left eye on the left, in a picture twice
as wide whose pixels are half as wide: shown at its whole width it has
the shape of one view, which is what a picture with both eyes side by
side is taken to be, so that the renderer and the stereoscopic modes
handle it as they handle any such video. The picture says "left_right"
itself. A packet gives a picture, as with any other stream.

Which pictures belong together, and the rest that is decided, is in
KODI::MULTIVIEW with its host test: two pictures are paired only when
they are named as different views and have the same time and size. A
base view whose second view does not come goes out alone, in both
halves; a second view without its base view is never shown; two
pictures that the decoder gives different times never go out as one.

It fails closed, to the base view as it played before:
- the decoder is not in the build, does not take the option, or cannot
  be opened: the stream is opened with the decoder of every other
  stream, in the same call;
- while playing: no second view ever came in 240 pictures, the decoder
  refused 48 packets in a row, a picture could not be made, or the
  pictures need a filter graph (deinterlacing, rotation, a pixel format
  that the renderer does not take: the graph is set up from the decoder
  context, which does not describe the wider picture). The stream is
  opened again with the decoder of every other stream, and with the
  hardware decoding that this codec does through FFmpeg, and the player
  sends the packets since the last key frame again;
- in both cases the pictures say "mono", so that nothing takes a half
  of a base view for an eye, and one line in the log says why.
CDVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME does not take such a stream.

A stream without the mark goes through the code it went through: the
new branches are all behind the mark or behind the packer that only
such a stream has.

Not covered: interlaced video of two views plays its base view where it
is deinterlaced, which is the default (it needs the filter); a stream
with more than two views plays its base view once the 240 pictures are
over.

Decoding both views by setting "view_ids" and showing them side by side
in one picture is what Dom Cobley (popcornmix) does in his Kodi branch
fix-stereoscopic-3d-gbm-upstream, there with a filter graph (hstack);
the code here is written anew.

Read and compiled; not run: the decoder is not in the last build, and
there is no stream of two views here.
